// chem — chemical-structure drawing, written entirely in the document language,
// followed by a demonstration of every capability. Render with:
//   texish scripts/chem.script
//
// The macros below are the whole library: bonds between named coordinates,
// shortened at each end so atom labels stay clear, and \atom to name-and-label a
// point. Bond geometry is computed with \xof/\yof and \calc over the picture
// primitives — no engine code. Tunable through bondgap (clearance at each end) and
// doublesep (half the gap between the two lines of a double bond).
\set bondgap {9}
\set doublesep {2.5}

// \bond{A}{B} — a single bond between two coordinates, shortened by bondgap at each end.
\def bond a b {
  \set chemAx {\xof{\a}} \set chemAy {\yof{\a}}
  \set chemBx {\xof{\b}} \set chemBy {\yof{\b}}
  \set chemLen {\calc{hypot(chemBx - chemAx, chemBy - chemAy)}}
  \set chemUx {\calc{(chemBx - chemAx) / chemLen}}
  \set chemUy {\calc{(chemBy - chemAy) / chemLen}}
  \line{(chemAx + chemUx*bondgap, chemAy + chemUy*bondgap)
        (chemBx - chemUx*bondgap, chemBy - chemUy*bondgap)}
}

// \dbond{A}{B} — a double bond: two parallel lines, offset doublesep either side of the bond axis.
\def dbond a b {
  \set chemAx {\xof{\a}} \set chemAy {\yof{\a}}
  \set chemBx {\xof{\b}} \set chemBy {\yof{\b}}
  \set chemLen {\calc{hypot(chemBx - chemAx, chemBy - chemAy)}}
  \set chemUx {\calc{(chemBx - chemAx) / chemLen}}
  \set chemUy {\calc{(chemBy - chemAy) / chemLen}}
  \set chemOx {\calc{-chemUy * doublesep}}
  \set chemOy {\calc{chemUx * doublesep}}
  \line{(chemAx + chemUx*bondgap + chemOx, chemAy + chemUy*bondgap + chemOy)
        (chemBx - chemUx*bondgap + chemOx, chemBy - chemUy*bondgap + chemOy)}
  \line{(chemAx + chemUx*bondgap - chemOx, chemAy + chemUy*bondgap - chemOy)
        (chemBx - chemUx*bondgap - chemOx, chemBy - chemUy*bondgap - chemOy)}
}

// \tbond{A}{B} — a triple bond: a central line flanked by two parallel lines a little wider apart.
\def tbond a b {
  \set chemAx {\xof{\a}} \set chemAy {\yof{\a}}
  \set chemBx {\xof{\b}} \set chemBy {\yof{\b}}
  \set chemLen {\calc{hypot(chemBx - chemAx, chemBy - chemAy)}}
  \set chemUx {\calc{(chemBx - chemAx) / chemLen}}
  \set chemUy {\calc{(chemBy - chemAy) / chemLen}}
  \set chemOx {\calc{-chemUy * doublesep * 1.8}}
  \set chemOy {\calc{chemUx * doublesep * 1.8}}
  \line{(chemAx + chemUx*bondgap, chemAy + chemUy*bondgap)
        (chemBx - chemUx*bondgap, chemBy - chemUy*bondgap)}
  \line{(chemAx + chemUx*bondgap + chemOx, chemAy + chemUy*bondgap + chemOy)
        (chemBx - chemUx*bondgap + chemOx, chemBy - chemUy*bondgap + chemOy)}
  \line{(chemAx + chemUx*bondgap - chemOx, chemAy + chemUy*bondgap - chemOy)
        (chemBx - chemUx*bondgap - chemOx, chemBy - chemUy*bondgap - chemOy)}
}

// \atom{name}{coord}{label} — name a coordinate and set its element label, centred on the point.
\def atom n c l {
  \coordinate{\n}{\c}
  \at anchor:center {\c}{\l}
}

\include scripts/logos.script

\def headline {\italic{chem} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}

\font lmroman 12 regular

// keep natural spacing between blocks; collect leftover space at the page bottom
// rather than stretching the gaps to fill the page (the default)
\set raggedbottom {1}

\bold{Drawing chemistry}

These macros draw chemical structures. Name an
atom with \italic{\\atom}, then connect named atoms with \italic{\\bond} (single),
\italic{\\dbond} (double), or \italic{\\tbond} (triple). Every bond is shortened
at both ends so the atom labels stay clear. The macros are written entirely in
\TeXish: their bond geometry is computed with \italic{\\xof}, \italic{\\yof}, and
\italic{\\calc} over the picture primitives — no engine code.

\bold{Atoms and single bonds — water}

\italic{\\atom} places a centred element label and names the point, and
\italic{\\bond} joins two named atoms by their names.

\picture width:2in height:1.4in {
  \translate{72 34}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{O}{(0, 44)}{O}
  \atom{Ha}{(-36, 4)}{H}
  \atom{Hb}{(36, 4)}{H}
  \bond{(O)}{(Ha)}
  \bond{(O)}{(Hb)}
}

\bold{Double bonds — carbon dioxide}

\italic{\\dbond} draws two parallel lines. Here the linear O=C=O of carbon
dioxide, both bonds double.

\picture width:3in height:0.8in {
  \translate{34 30}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{O1}{(0, 0)}{O}
  \atom{C}{(72, 0)}{C}
  \atom{O2}{(144, 0)}{O}
  \dbond{(O1)}{(C)}
  \dbond{(C)}{(O2)}
}

\bold{Triple bonds — acetylene}

\italic{\\tbond} draws a triple bond — three parallel lines. Acetylene is
H–C≡C–H, a triple bond between the carbons.

\picture width:3in height:0.7in {
  \translate{20 26}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{Ha}{(0, 0)}{H}
  \atom{C1}{(54, 0)}{C}
  \atom{C2}{(120, 0)}{C}
  \atom{Hb}{(174, 0)}{H}
  \bond{(Ha)}{(C1)}
  \tbond{(C1)}{(C2)}
  \bond{(C2)}{(Hb)}
}

\bold{Rings — benzene}

Coordinates make rings easy: place the carbons and hydrogens on two polar rings,
then loop the bonds, alternating single and double around the ring.

\picture width:2.7in height:2.6in {
  \translate{97 94}
  \set R {46} \set HR {86}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \for\k{\range{0}{5}}{
    \set a {\calc{k*60}} \set b {\calc{(k+1)*60}}
    \if{\calc{k % 2}}
      \bond{(a:R)}{(b:R)}
    \else
      \dbond{(a:R)}{(b:R)}
    \fi
    \bond{(a:R)}{(a:HR)}
    \at anchor:center {(a:R)}{C}
    \at anchor:center {(a:HR)}{H}
  }
}

\bold{Tuning}

The clearance at each bond end and the gap of a multiple bond are the variables
\italic{bondgap} and \italic{doublesep}; set them to restyle every bond. Below,
the same ethene C=C drawn at the defaults and then with a wider double-bond gap.

\picture width:3in height:0.9in {
  \translate{30 30}
  \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.2pt}
  \atom{C1}{(0, 0)}{C}  \atom{C2}{(72, 0)}{C}
  \dbond{(C1)}{(C2)}
  \set doublesep {5}
  \atom{C3}{(150, 0)}{C}  \atom{C4}{(222, 0)}{C}
  \dbond{(C3)}{(C4)}
}
